00:00:13.347 --> 00:00:15.305
[SQUEAKING] [RUSTLING]
[CLICKING] MARKUS KLUTE:

00:00:15.305 --> 00:00:16.820
Welcome back to 8.701.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.010
We're starting a new
chapter now, Chapter 9,

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.090
on nuclear physics.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.700
And this video is the
first introduction

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.890
into the topic,
where I'm explaining

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.000
some of the terminology
and some of the concepts.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.660
We dive in much more
detail as we go on.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.108
So given an atom, you can
specify the number of neutrons,

00:00:38.108 --> 00:00:39.650
the number of protons,
and the number

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.410
of electrons, which is equal
to the number of protons,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.970
for neutral atoms.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.450
Atoms of the same element, they
have the same atomic number,

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:52.630
Z, but they're not all the same.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.480
Isotopes of the same element
have different numbers

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.690
of neutrons.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:03.640
So we can have uranium with
a number of neutrons varying.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.500
You typically write an
isotope by specifying

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.250
the mass, the number of protons,
and the number of neutrons.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.060
But that information
is redundant.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.500
So typically, we simplify
this by just writing things

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.260
like 238 uranium,
and that specifies

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.760
a specific isotope of uranium.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.750
When talking about
different nuclei,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.960
we sometimes refer
to them as nuclide,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.920
atom/nucleus with a
specific number of neutrons

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.860
and a specific
number of protons.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.970
Isobars are nuclides
with the same mass,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:51.660
with the same number, same
sum of protons and neutrons,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.800
but with varying individual
number of protons and neutrons.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.550
An isotine is a nuclide with
the same number of neutrons,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.040
but with varying
number of protons,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.320
and an isomer is
the same nuclide

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.430
but different
[? eigen ?] states,

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.760
which means that the
energy states are--

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:09.850
an energy state.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.980
So we can excite nuclides at
their component particles.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.330
The nuclear radius
typically can be extracted

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.440
from the mass of the nuclide.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.600
And it's simply we add
little balls to the sum,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.010
and it scales with A to the
1/3 the mass of a number

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.920
of elements in this nuclide.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.540
There's many isotopes.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.050
There's many nuclides.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:44.390
And so you typically can look
at all of them, if you want,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.510
or a subset of them
in nuclear charts

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:51.355
that's given here, where we
plot here the number of protons,

00:02:51.355 --> 00:02:52.825
and here the number of neutrons.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.300
And we look at many more
of those charts later.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.810
Here's another representation
of the very same thing.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.180
You see a nuclear chart again.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.180
And here, what's spotted in
red are the stable nuclei.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.510
We will see that
nuclei can decay,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.390
and we'll understand why
they decay and in what form

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:16.170
they decay.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.150
It's a core part
of this chapter,

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.330
understanding how
nuclei can decay,

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:26.430
and what we can learn about
them by studying their decays.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.030
One way to look at
it, for example,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.930
we see that here I
plot Z over A, so

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:36.730
the number of protons over the
sum of the number of protons, Z

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:41.740
plus N. And you see that
most of the stable nuclei,

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.610
with the exception of the one
with very small mass number,

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:52.240
have less protons than neutrons.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.670
So there's an
excess of neutrons.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.380
This can also be seen here.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.750
The stable nuclei are
typically on or below this axis

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.940
where Z is equal to A.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.980
Radioactive decays can be
characterized, typically,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.430
as a parent nuclide, and
then a daughter nuclide.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.640
And so radioactive
decay is a process

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.580
in which an unstable nucleus
spontaneously loses energy

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.459
by emitting
particles, ionization

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.709
particles and radiation.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.620
The decay and the loss
of energy results, then,

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.250
in an atom of one type, the
parent particle or parent

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:32.662
nuclide, transforming into
another type of an atom,

00:04:32.662 --> 00:04:34.650
or the daughter nuclide.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.340
We have already
looked at decay rate

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.520
in the concept of particle
physics interactions,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.400
but we can define this very
similar here, the decay rate,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.280
or sometimes it is
called decay constant.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.110
And then as we
did before, we can

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.920
define the mean lifetime or the
half life of a parent nuclide.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.460
So this is it for
the introduction.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:06.530
And in the next lecture, we'll
start looking in the energy

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:10.720
which is used to bind
the nuclei, the protons

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.990
and neutrons together, and
how we can understand this

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.950
from an empirical model.

